  "feature_availability": "scaled_temperature and optical_proxy are observed before the response assay, including for future batch F. scaled_temperature_squared means the square of the same row temperature, no refit or independent measurement. post_assay_quality_index is computed only after measuring that same row response, is not available on future samples and may not be substituted by a fitted value. The candidate model using it is scientifically ineligible; high training accuracy does not override availability.",
  "learning_rule": "For each eligible candidate use ridge regression with alpha=1 exactly, an unpenalized intercept, and no other regularization or imputation. For EVERY fit, form that candidate feature matrix, fit population standard deviations (ddof=0) and means to that fit training rows ONLY, map a zero scale to 1, standardize training and prediction rows by those values, then minimize sum squared residuals + sum squared non-intercept coefficients in standardized coordinates. Response is not standardized.",
  "model_selection": "Use only partition=training. Perform exact leave-ONE-BATCH-out cross-validation over the six named T batches. Within each fold all rows of the held-out batch are withheld from fitting AND scaling. Score a candidate by the unweighted mean across six held-out batches of that batch mean squared prediction error. Select the smallest score, resolving differences <=1e-10 by lexicographic candidate ID. Do not tune on calibration or future labels. Refit the selected candidate once on all T training rows, including refitting its scaler, and keep it fixed thereafter.",
  "calibration_assumptions": "Conditional on training, the nine complete K batch blocks and ONE future batch block are exchangeable. Each has four jointly observed specimens; dependence within a batch is arbitrary. Region is a recorded covariate, not a guarantee that calibration residual distributions are equal conditional on region. The future batch is coastal. No conditional-on-region coverage theorem or independence of specimens is granted. The dataset is one realization; interval guarantees follow only under these expressly assumed sampling conditions.",
  "calibration_rule": "For each K batch, score = maximum over its four specimens of absolute error from the fixed training-only selected model. For nominal simultaneous coverage 1-alpha for ALL four response values in ONE new exchangeable batch, set k=ceil((n+1)*(1-alpha)) where n is number of calibration batches, and q=the kth smallest batch score if k<=n; otherwise q=+infinity. Intervals are [prediction-q,prediction+q] for each requested future row. This finite-rank split-conformal rule includes ties; do not interpolate a sample percentile. Report the 90% global-batch q and interval bounds. For an infinite radius, report null, not a fabricated large finite value.",
  "audit_proposals": {
    "A": "Keep the training-only fitted model, use one maximum residual per calibration batch, and apply the declared n=9 batch rank at alpha=0.1. Claim at least 90% marginal probability that all four responses in ONE future batch lie in their intervals, under stated exchangeability.",
    "B": "Keep the same training-only fit, pool all 36 individual calibration residuals, apply ceil((36+1)*0.9), and claim at least 90% probability that all four future responses lie in their intervals under the stated arbitrary within-batch dependence.",
    "C": "After looking at K responses, choose whichever eligible candidate has the smallest 90% maximum-batch residual quantile; refit its predictor including K labels and reuse those same K labels as split-conformal calibration. Claim the same theorem without any further adjustment.",
    "D": "Use proposal A and claim at least 90% coverage conditional on coastal region merely because future features include that region.",
    "E": "Keep the fixed training-only model but calibrate ONLY on coastal K batches and assume coastal blocks exchangeable with future coastal block. Apply the same finite-rank rule at alpha=0.1. Claim a finite 90% radius from those blocks.",
    "F": "Keep the model and all nine K batch scores. For simultaneous coverage at least 90% over TWO future batch blocks, use Bonferroni alpha=0.05 per batch with the same finite-rank rule. Dependence between the two future blocks is unrestricted. Claim that the resulting radius is finite."
